WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.880
 Hello guys, in this video we will talk about TF-IDF and how it should be calculated with

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:22.800
 a very simple example. So, let's start. A very popular representation for text is

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.600
 a product of term frequency and inverse document frequency, commonly referred to as TF-IDF.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:39.240
 The TF-IDF value of a term T in a given document D is like this. Note that a TF-IDF value is

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:46.720
 specific to a single document D, where IDF depends on the entire corpus. Systems employing the

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.840
 back of words representation typically go through steps of stemming and stop words elimination

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:59.320
 before doing term counts. Term counts within the document from the TF values

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:06.440
 for each term and the document counts across the corpus from the IDF values. Each document

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:12.920
 thus becomes a feature vector and the corpus is set of these feature vectors. This can be

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.560
 used in a data mining algorithm for classification, clustering or retrieval.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:25.880
 That was a quick introduction to TF-IDF and now let's go to a simple task. Let's have a situation.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:34.520
 We have some sentences. The first one is: A quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. What a fox!

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:42.120
 The second one: A quick brown fox jumps over the lazy fox. What a fox!

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:53.160
 Please keep in mind that all the sentences in our corpus are defined as small d. So we have D1 and D2.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:02:04.040
 Based on this rule, our corpus is defined as the big D. Now we have some data. This data led us to shape a main question.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:18.200
 And the question is like this: How word fox is relevant to corpus-D documents? Remember we have documents D1 and D2.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:26.360
 Let's go to the solution part. Let's start in here with some definitions. What is DFIDF?

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:35.000
 In the first part of this calculation we need to clarify that DF is the frequency of any term in a given

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:43.880
 document. We need to calculate DF for document number one and document number two by a given

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:55.160
 argument, a word fox. So let's calculate. For document number one we have 12 words in total. In this context,

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:03.640
 we have a word fox occurred two times. Knowing this information we can calculate DF like this.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:17.240
 2 divided by 12 equals 0.17. In the same way we calculate DF for document number two. In this case,

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:24.760
 we have fox occurred three times in this document. So the calculation will be as follow.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:38.280
 3 divided by 12 equal to 0.25. Keeping in mind that D1 and D2 has the same number of total words.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:46.040
 The first part of calculation is done. Now we have to move to the second part. We need to calculate

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:55.960
 IDF. IDF. IDF is constant per corpus and account for the ratio of documents that include data specific term.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:04.920
 We need to calculate IDF for full corpus that we have. For this we are using a logarithm.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:16.520
 In this equation, at the upper side, we need to look at how many documents at our corpus a given word fox is accurate.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:25.160
 Now we see that award fox is accurate in document number one and in document number two.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:32.360
 So, at the upper side of our equation, we need to input a value of two.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:41.160
 On the lower side of this equation, we have to input a value of total documents that our corpus consists of.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:49.560
 And that means we have to input value of two because we have two documents in total in our corpus.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:57.960
 That resulting into logarithm 2 divided by two and it is equal to zero.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:06.840
 Now we have enough information to calculate TF IDF for all documents in our corpus D.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:17.800
 We have document number one and document number two. So, we will calculate TF IDF separately for each document in our corpus.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:30.680
 For the first document, TF IDF equal to 0.17 from the first part of our calculation multiplied by 0.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.920
 It is from second part of our calculation.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.640
 And it is equal to zero.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:43.320
 For the second document, we calculate TF IDF in the same rules that we applied for the first document.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:48.760
 Let's do like this. 0.25 from the first part of our calculation.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:54.840
 And multiplied by 0 from the second part of our calculations.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.320
 And it is equal to zero.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:04.040
 Now we have calculated TF IDF for all documents in our corpus.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:09.000
 And for document number one, TF IDF equal to zero.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:16.280
 For the second document in our corpus, we have calculated TF IDF and it is equal to zero again.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:22.040
 Now we have calculated TF IDF for all the documents in our corpus.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:26.760
 And that means that now we can answer to the main question in this task.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.320
 How a word fox is relevant to corpus D documents?

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:41.880
 And the answer is: Using TF IDF that we have calculated just before, the word fox is equally relevant

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:49.480
 for both documents D1 and document D2 because we have the same values of TF IDF.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:50.920
 It's zero.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:57.880
 This calculation can be applied in any amount of documents that you have in your corpus.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:03.880
 And one more time again, what is TF and IDF?

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:10.440
 TF is a simple choice to use the raw count of a term in a document.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:17.960
 And the IDF, inverse document frequency, is a measurement of how much information the word

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.200
 provides in our corpus.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:25.000
 By saying corpus, I mean across all the documents that we are having.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:32.280
 So by summarizing this video, TF IDF is a statistical measurement that evaluates how

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:36.120
 relevant a word is to a document in a collection of documents.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:42.520
 This is done by multiplying two metrics: how many times a word appears in a document,

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:47.000
 and the inverse document frequency of the words across the set of documents.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:55.640
 It has many uses, most importantly in automated text analysis, and is very useful for scoring words

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.600
 in machine learning algorithms for NLP.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:03.880
 TF IDF was invented for document search and information retrieval.

